﻿# Coolsecture: An Easy-to-Use and Improved Framework for Cross-Species Hi-C Contact Map Comparison

Coolsecture provides a modernized, user-friendly framework for comparative Hi-C analysis across species. By adopting standardized inputs and outputs, improving the statistical treatment of contact scores, implementing vectorized and parallel algorithms, and supplying comprehensive diagnostic visualizations, Coolsecture addresses the limitations of the original [C-InterSecture](https://github.com/NuriddinovMA/C-InterSecture) pipeline.

---

## Requirements

Coolsecture requires **Python 3.8+** and the following dependencies:

- [minimap2](https://github.com/lh3/minimap2)  
- numpy  
- pandas  
- matplotlib  
- [cooler](https://github.com/open2c/cooler)
- h5py  

Optional:
- hic-straw (Python backend for .hic conversion)
- juicer_tools (optional, for .hic output in lift2matrix)
- plotly (optional, for interactive HTML visualizations)

---

## Installation

Clone this repository and install in editable mode:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/pk-zhu/Coolsecture.git && cd Coolsecture
pip install -e .
```

For interactive Plotly outputs:

```bash
pip install -e .[viz]
```

---

## Usage

To view the help message:

```bash
coolsecture -h
coolsecture <subcommand> -h
```

Notes:
- `prepare` accepts `.hic` input via `hic2cool`. Use `--resolution` to specify resolution in bp (required for `.hic`; comma-separated values are supported for `.mcool`).
- `prepare` uses `--matrix` and supports multi-resolution benchmarks on `.mcool` via comma-separated `--resolution` values. Add `--summary` to write a summary TSV and plot (runtime, nnz, and coverage metrics).
- `link2mark` accepts UCSC `.chain` via `--chain`.
- `lift2matrix` can output `.cool` (default) and optionally `.hic` using the default `juicer_tools` command if available.
- `run-all` provides an end-to-end pipeline (no plot-cross) from FASTA + contact matrices; resolution is required.
- `liftcontracts` runs A->B and B->A liftover and reports reciprocal consistency metrics and uncertainty tags.
- `multiscale` summarizes multi-resolution PBAD stability and coarse/fine divergence tags.
- `cross-validate` computes stratum-adjusted correlation (HiCRep-style) between matched source-coordinate percentile matrices (typically `Observed` and `Control` from `lift2matrix`); resolution must match.
- Most summary tables can emit interactive Plotly HTML outputs; use `--interactive on|off|auto` (default: auto) where available.
- The example Snakemake pipeline enforces **PDF-only** visualization outputs and removes any Plotly HTML artifacts by design.

---

## Interactive Visualizations

Summary tables produced by the following commands can generate interactive Plotly HTML files (responsive, with labels and legends):

- `prepare --summary` (multi-resolution summary)
- `bidirectional` (summary + tag distribution)
- `bidirectional` (summary + tag distribution)
- `multiscale` (PBAD summary + stability tags)
- `cross-validate` (SCC vs distance)

The HTML outputs are written alongside the TSV outputs with a `.plotly.html` suffix.
See `docs/VISUALIZATIONS.md` for implementation details.

---

## CI

A lightweight CI workflow compiles the Python sources and verifies the CLI can start:
- `python -m compileall src`
- `python -m coolsecture -h`

### Updating Visualizations with New Data

1. Re-run the same command with updated inputs (new matrices, new liftover tables, or new resolutions).
2. Keep the same `--out-prefix` to overwrite existing TSV/HTML outputs, or change it to retain versioned snapshots.
3. If Plotly is installed, the interactive HTML files update automatically when `--interactive auto` or `--interactive on` is used.

If you need to disable Plotly generation in automated pipelines, pass `--interactive off`.

---

## Example Workflow

1. Navigate to the example directory:

```bash
cd example
```

2. Download example data and place it in the cools directory. For instance, obtain the .mcool files from the [Figureshare](https://github.com/pk-zhu/Coolsecture) and put them under cools:

```bash
tree .
example/
鈹溾攢鈹€ config.yaml
鈹溾攢鈹€ cools
鈹?  鈹溾攢鈹€ Asu.mcool
鈹?  鈹斺攢鈹€ Ath.mcool
鈹溾攢鈹€ Snakefile
鈹斺攢鈹€ step0
    鈹溾攢鈹€ Asu_Ath.link
    鈹溾攢鈹€ Asu.fa.fai
    鈹斺攢鈹€ Ath.fa.fai
```

3. Run a dry-run to preview the workflow and Execute the full workflow

```bash
snakemake -np
snakemake -j 8
```

---

## Multi-resolution Batch Workflow (Manual CLI)

Run `prepare` once to generate multiple resolutions:

```bash
coolsecture prepare \
  --matrix Asu.mcool \
  --resolution 10000,20000,40000,80000 \
  --summary \
  --out-prefix step1/Asu/Asu
```

Then lift contacts in batch using the prefixes:

```bash
coolsecture bidirectional \
  --matrix-a-prefix step1/Asu/Asu \
  --matrix-b-prefix step1/Ath/Ath \
  --faidx-a Asu.fa.fai --faidx-b Ath.fa.fai \
  --mark-ab step2/Asu_Ath.mark \
  --out-prefix step2/Asu_Ath/Asu_Ath
```

Downstream batch processing:

```bash
coolsecture contact-stat --liftover-prefix step2/Asu_Ath/Asu_Ath --faidx Asu.fa.fai --out-prefix step3/Asu_Ath
coolsecture metric       --liftover-prefix step2/Asu_Ath/Asu_Ath --faidx Asu.fa.fai --out-prefix step3/Asu_Ath
coolsecture plot-cross   --liftover-prefix step2/Asu_Ath/Asu_Ath --faidx Asu.fa.fai --locus chr1:0-10000000 --out-prefix step3/Asu_Ath
coolsecture lift2matrix  --liftover-prefix step2/Asu_Ath/Asu_Ath --faidx Asu.fa.fai --out-prefix step3/Asu_Ath
```

---

## Run-All (End-to-End, No plot-cross)

Provide FASTA and matrices for both species and a required resolution:

```bash
coolsecture run-all \
  --genome-a Asu.fa \
  --genome-b Ath.fa \
  --matrix-a Asu.mcool \
  --matrix-b Ath.mcool \
  --resolution 40000 \
  --out-prefix run_all
```

Optional pass-through arguments:
`--prepare-args`, `--link2mark-args`, `--bidirectional-args`, `--contact-stat-args`, `--metric-args`, `--lift2matrix-args`.

---

## Bidirectional Liftover (Reciprocal Metrics)

```bash
coolsecture bidirectional \
  --contact-a step1/Asu/Asu.contacts.tsv \
  --contact-b step1/Ath/Ath.contacts.tsv \
  --faidx-a Asu.fa.fai --faidx-b Ath.fa.fai \
  --mark-ab step2/Asu_Ath.mark \
  --out-prefix step2/Asu_Ath/Asu_Ath
```

Outputs:
- `*.bidirectional.summary.tsv` (reciprocal consistency, symmetry, Reciprocal PBAD, conservation score)
- `*.bidirectional.tags.tsv` (uncertainty tags per contact)

---

## Multi-scale PBAD Summary

```bash
coolsecture multiscale \
  --liftover-prefix step2/Asu_Ath/Asu_Ath \
  --faidx Asu.fa.fai \
  --out-prefix step3/Asu_Ath
```

Outputs:
- `*.multiscale.pbad.tsv` (resolution, mean/median PBAD, high-PBAD fraction)
- `*.multiscale.summary.tsv` (resolution stability + coarse/fine tags)

---

## Cross-Validate (HiCRep-style SCC)

Compare matched source-coordinate percentile matrices from `lift2matrix` at the same resolution (typically `Observed.cool` vs `Control.cool`):

```bash
coolsecture cross-validate \
  --matrix-a step3/Asu_Ath.Observed.cool \
  --matrix-b step3/Asu_Ath.Control.cool \
  --max-dist-mb 10 \
  --out-prefix step3/Asu_Ath
```

Notes:
- `matrix-a` and `matrix-b` must have the same resolution.
- For statistical comparability, `matrix-a` and `matrix-b` should encode the same value type and coordinates; using the paired `Observed`/`Control` outputs from one `lift2matrix` run is recommended.
- Outputs: `*.scc.tsv` and `*.scc.png|pdf`.

